"""Research report producer. Does not modify graph or accepted packets."""
import json,hashlib
from pathlib import Path
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
context=json.loads(Path('/tmp/response-edge-node-context-debates.json').read_text())
def write(name,obj): (HERE/name).write_text(json.dumps(obj,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
notes='''
wittgenstein|Geertz's borrowing concerns later public meaning; Ryle's comparison and Skinner's use are distinct. Earlier logical work cannot replace those qualifications. No further named response was verified.
ryle|Geertz, Freud, Wittgenstein and Dunn have differentiated routes. A Cartesian response needs a suitable existing endpoint and a checked argument; comparison alone is not transmission.
geertz|Asad, Hunt, Hobsbawm, Stone and Huang already supply criticisms; Levi-Strauss and Weber are also represented. Another generic objection to interpretation would not identify a new response.
saussure|This is a structural-linguistics group. Derrida's criticism and Meillet, Benveniste, Jakobson and Trubetzkoy contexts cannot all become an individual Saussure genealogy.
levistrauss|Derrida, Braudel, Lacan and Hall/Hebdige routes already distinguish responses. The Mauss/Durkheim relation is mixed; a sole Mauss debt needs a separately bounded claim.
barthes|Bann's criticism and Marx/Engels, Nietzsche, Vernant and Hebdige contexts differ. S/Z is a possible further reading task, not an edge established by bibliographic presence.
lacan|Freud, structural linguistics, Levi-Strauss, Althusser and Scott's scoped gender criticism are represented. Butler's Lacan discussion remains an unread-passage lead here.
althusser|Thompson's explicit criticism, Hall's mixed discussion and Gramsci, Freud, Lacan, Foucault and epistemology routes already register disagreement. Generic anti-humanism would duplicate rather than specify it.
nietzsche|Foucault, Barthes, Koselleck, Benjamin and Burckhardt contexts are different uses. An absent thinker cannot be replaced by an arbitrary collective endpoint.
epistemology|Canguilhem's Foucault connection differs from Bachelard-related geography and Halbwachs contexts. Althusser and science-history routes do not make this collective a single author.
foucault|Derrida, Hall, Chartier, Said, Spivak and de Certeau already criticize specific arguments. Fresh Butler, Ginzburg and Agamben passages establish distinct omissions, each attributed to its critic rather than presented as a definitive refutation.
derrida|Saussure, Levi-Strauss, Foucault, Freud, archives and Spivak already have differentiated routes. The Austin/Searle controversy needs appropriate endpoints and primary reading, not a generic language target.
ranke|Lamprecht, Wehler, Croce/Droysen, Salmon, Tuchman and Gu Jiegang already contest critical scholarship. The tradition endpoint is not interchangeable with Ranke personally.
karl|Thompson, Wood's Cohen criticism, Weber's qualification, Marx and Williams already differentiate materialist history. Arendt's new argument belongs to Marx's person entry, not the entire tradition.
durkheim|Mauss/Hubert, Simiand, Halbwachs and historians' uses are differently attributed. Latour's personal Durkheim criticism was already repaired; genuinely collective routes remain collective.
weber|Interpretive method, historical domination/state inquiry and Frankfurt rationalization are three different routes. Personal Weber criticisms also occur at max_weber; the group's low degree is not proof of omission.
historicalecon|Schmoller/Rogers, Menger's reply, Toynbee's conditional deduction and Weber on Knies already represent methodological disputes. Ashley's editing and institutional roles are not equivalent to theoretical agreement.
economictheory|Marx on Smith/Ricardo and Wakefield, Polanyi on Smith, dependency criticisms and Guha's colonial inquiry are present. Shared economic vocabulary does not establish another influence.
geography|Harvey/Massey, Hayden, imperial geography and epistemological resources are represented. Harvey's Lefebvre engagement is promising, but its precise primary argument remains a reading hold.
oldculture|Lamprecht's Ranke dispute, Huizinga and Burckhardt/Nietzsche contexts do not establish continuous inheritance to every later cultural historian.
lifetradition|Woolf/Strachey and Robbe-Grillet-related criticism distinguish literary form from methodological resource. Bourdieu's critique does not turn all literary biography into one school.
wartradition|Delbruck, Beloch, Howard/Paret, Keegan/Bassford and Liddell Hart/Howard already mark different resources and disputes. A generic military-theory relation would flatten their particular claims.
anthropology|Asad/Geertz, Duerr/Elias, Blok/Hobsbawm, Wilson and Wolfe already register disagreement. Deloria's anthropologist critique remains a priority chapter-reading lead, not a conclusion from its title.
nationaltradition|Chatterjee, Guha, Thapar, Liang, Beard, Hobsbawm and Catherine Hall criticize different national narratives. These cannot become a universal rejection of national history.
identity|Hall, Gilroy, Scott, Crenshaw, Taylor and Brubaker have different accounts. The new Brubaker/Cooper response concerns Hall's analytical indispensability claim, not the illegitimacy of political identification.
culture|Sewell/Skocpol and Levi/Darnton already supply disagreement among many contributions. Co-presence in cultural history does not establish transmission between contributors.
language|Palmer, Dorothy Thompson, Chartier, Lawrence and Canning contest distinct linguistic approaches. Narrative, discourse and language do not identify one uniform postmodernist opponent.
subaltern|Guha, Spivak, Nair, Chakrabarty and gender criticisms mark changes within the collective. Spivak's Deleuze response is her own argument, not the collective's unanimous position.
gender|Scott, Fraser, Oyewumi, Frevert and materialist disputes already qualify the field. Butler's new Foucault criticism is individual; possible Lacan criticism remains a separate reading task.
queer|Foucault, Stoler, D'Emilio, Cohen and Butler occupy distinct positions. Butler's Foucault criticism complicates agreement without becoming a verdict by queer history collectively.
post|Chakrabarty, Mohanty, Harris and settler-colonial disputes already contest the field. McClintock's Said criticism is present; Bhabha/Fanon requires actual passages beyond contents metadata.
memory|Halbwachs, Ricoeur, Todorov, Portelli, Nora, Assmann and Samuel represent different projects. A Ricoeur-Nora or Ricoeur-Halbwachs response cannot follow merely from shared terminology.
freud|Anna and Sigmund Freud, Marcuse/Fromm and Gay/Erikson/deMause have different roles. Scott's use does not identify one Freud text; collective qualifications remain necessary.
latour|Bloor's criticism and Latour's reply, science wars, Kuhn-related comparison and Durkheim already provide explicit opposition. Chakrabarty's historical use is a different claim.
ssk|Bloor/Latour, science wars and Kuhn's objections already distinguish the strong programme from all science studies. Methodological relativism is not automatically opposition to science.
conceptual|Koselleck, context-oriented work, Hartog, political/social history and Elias already connect the field. Different methodological labels do not establish a Skinner-Koselleck dispute.
fanon|Freud, Hall, Catherine Hall and criticism of national elites are represented. Bhabha's Fanon reading is important to investigate but requires a specific primary argument.
gramsci|Hall, Althusser, Hobsbawm, Said, Guha, McKay and Eley/Fraser show differentiated uses. Scott is not automatically Gramscian because both address subordinate politics.
historicism|Skinner's Collingwood criticism, Benjamin, Chakrabarty and Croce/Droysen already distinguish disagreements. The group does not denote one unchanged historicism.
philhistory|The White criticism mediated through Ricoeur, Carr, Lorenz, Ankersmit and Runia is collective. Bloch, Prost, Veyne, Ferguson, Arendt and Brown ask different questions; a direct Ricoeur-White response needs fresh primary evidence.
frankfurt|Institutional Halbwachs/Tawney contexts differ from Habermas, Benjamin and Adorno contributions. The new Adorno-Benjamin letter supplies internal disagreement while retaining shared aims.
practice|Bourdieu, de Certeau, Giddens, Goffman, Scott and Swidler/Sewell do not form one theory. De Certeau's direct criticism of habitus/reproduction is a verified missing response.
elias|Weber/Freud comparisons are not all influence; Anna/Sigmund Freud and Durkheim remain qualified. Duerr and Rosenwein already criticize Elias. Douglas's answer passages require further reading.
culturalstudies|Hall, Williams, Adorno/Horkheimer, Carby and feminists contribute different projects. The Adorno letter is not a collective verdict; Hall's Two Paradigms needs separation from existing mixed routes before another personal edge.
nationalismstudies|Gellner/Smith criticism and reply, Hobsbawm on Gellner and Gellner/Hroch, and Anderson already represent distinct debates. A simplified modernism/ethnosymbolism edge would duplicate qualified disagreement.
truthdebate|White, Ginzburg, Hobsbawm, Cronon, Scott, science wars, Latour and SSK have differentiated claims. Ginzburg's new Foucault criticism concerns excluded people and usable hostile documents, not generic relativism.
linda_tuhiwai_smith|One Indigenous-research relation does not demonstrate complete coverage. A named response about Western historical knowledge is a priority primary-reading task; no specific new opponent was verified here.
racial_formation|Omi/Winant editions and later historical contributions are not interchangeable. Brubaker/Cooper explicitly criticize the 1994 ethnic/racial-group distinction; this bounded response is newly verified.
intersectionality|Crenshaw's contexts, Cohen and the racial-formation comparison distinguish argument from editorial comparison. A Crenshaw-Higginbotham genealogy needs a named debt, not resemblance.
vine_deloria_jr|The anthropology-response lead is substantial, but the entry says original chapters were unread. Anthropologists and Other Friends requires actual chapter reading before adding a critique from its title.
aldo_leopold|Environmental history, Cronon and Worster already record specific reception. They do not establish Leopold's founding authority over every environmental historian.
rachel_carson|The environmental route records reception rather than exhaustive impact. A personal Worster response needs his explicit passage; low degree alone is not verification.
sciencewars|Existing routes distinguish attackers, replies, the strong programme and historiographical readings. Neither Sokal nor broad scientific realism can stand for one unified collective actor.
edward_said|The Huntington criticism is already edge_1566. Marx, Foucault, Huang and McClintock contexts also exist. Recognizing these arguments freshly does not make them missing edges.
gayatri_chakravorty_spivak|Foucault, Guha, Derrida and named critics are represented. Her conversation critique explicitly addresses Deleuze too; his individual entry supports a separate bounded response while retaining the Foucault edge.
ellen_meiksins_wood|Brenner, Thompson, Cohen and Frank/Blaut already distinguish positive debts from opponents. Wallerstein cannot be inferred as a particular target from the general capitalism debate.
archival_turn|Stoler, Trouillot, Hartman, Fuentes, Derrida and Foucault have different accounts of archives. Shared archival exclusions do not establish interpersonal criticism; critical fabulation does not license invented evidence.
james_c_scott|Thompson's resource is already edge_1303 beside practice and legibility. A moral-economy relation could be distinct, but requires passages rather than duplicating the existing debt.
pierre_bourdieu|Historical receptions, Robbe-Grillet and Durkheim are specific. De Certeau praises descriptive ingenuity while questioning habitus/reproduction: an absent direct critique, not rejection of all practice theory.
jurgen_habermas|Fraser and Ryan already qualify the public sphere. A so-called Foucault-Habermas debate is not automatically an actual exchange; any new edge needs an authored argument.
hannah_arendt|Political history and truth are connected, but Human Condition directly criticizes Marx's labor/work and emancipation arguments. The proposal retains Arendt's respect for Marx rather than applying a generic anti-Marx label.
judith_butler|Gender and queer contributions omit the specific criticism of Foucault's Barbin presentation. That argument is verified; Lacan/Freud responses remain separate unread-passage tasks.
david_harvey|Urban, spatial and Massey routes exist. Lefebvre's right-to-the-city resource is plausible and important, but the accessible opening did not verify its exact scope in this review.
maurice_halbwachs|Memory and statistical/epistemological routes are distinct. Durkheim, Bergson or Bartlett relations need named engagement; co-membership or familiar genealogy is insufficient.
mary_louise_pratt|Postcolonial and Indigenous routes exist. Fresh Contact and Community passages use Anderson to question homogeneous speech-community models; this does not accuse Anderson of believing real nations homogeneous.
homi_k_bhabha|Postcolonial and global contributions are bounded. Fanon, Arendt and Benjamin are promising resources, but their relevant primary arguments were not freshly verified here.
max_weber|Tawney/Braudel criticism, Geertz and qualified materialist engagement already prevent a Weber-versus-Marx caricature. Historical-economic institutional context is not theoretical agreement.
paul_ricoeur|Memory and microhistory routes exist; White criticism is also represented collectively through a named reader. Direct White, Nora or Halbwachs responses need passages isolating Ricoeur's own argument.
karl_marx|Said, Hartz, political economy, Wakefield and materialist history already distinguish reworking and reception. Arendt's new labor/work response adds an argument without settling Marx interpretation.
stuart_hall|Gramsci, Fanon, Foucault, encoding and identity are specific. Brubaker/Cooper question analytical indispensability while recognizing Hall's anti-essentialism; both the coauthor and qualification must remain.
walter_benjamin|Nietzsche, Winter, historicism, Frankfurt and memory routes exist. Adorno's 1936 letter adds a specific disagreement about art and reception while preserving common aims.
henri_lefebvre|Spatial and capitalism routes exist. Harvey is a strong primary-reading lead, but shared urban interests alone cannot establish the proposed influence.
raymond_williams|Ordinary culture and criticism of prescriptive materialism exist. Hall's Two Paradigms may support a personal response only after differentiating it from the current mixed cultural-studies routes.
erving_goffman|Practice and identity concern interaction and presentation. A Bourdieu critique requires a passage naming Goffman's relevant argument, not a generic objection to interactionism.
rogers_brubaker|Identity and politics omit two explicit jointly authored responses: Hall's indispensability claim and Omi/Winant's ethnic/racial distinction. Both proposals retain Cooper and distinguish analytical criticism from political illegitimacy.
ernest_gellner|Hobsbawm and both directions of the Smith exchange already supply criticism. Smith's acknowledgment of modern transformation must not become a timeless-continuity caricature.
anne_mcclintock|Gender and direct Said criticism exist. Another Imperial Leather/Said edge would need a distinct argument rather than restating gender omission.
marcel_mauss|The gift and joint Hubert sacrifice work are distinct. Levi-Strauss/Durkheim-group routes remain mixed; personal attribution cannot erase Hubert or turn a collective setting into sole authorship.
mary_douglas|Body symbolism and joint Isherwood goods work exist. The 1987 conversation published in 2022 may supply Elias/Durkheim engagement, but editorial introduction and unread answer fragments are insufficient.
anthony_giddens|Practice and spatial contributions exist. Sewell's structure reformulation is a useful response lead, but an abstract alone cannot establish the disagreement or attribute it to all historical sociology.
gilles_deleuze|Foucault and technology routes retain collaborators' credits. Spivak explicitly criticizes his conversation position; this is not generalized to all Deleuze/Guattari work.
anthony_d_smith|The field contribution and both directions of the Gellner exchange capture the central dispute and qualification. Extra density alone would not add a new argument.
arjun_appadurai|Commodities and migration are connected. Anderson's imagined communities/worlds relation needs primary passages; similar terms alone do not establish it.
paul_gilroy|Cultural studies, identity, geography and Atlantic uses are differentiated. Du Bois's double-consciousness resource is a priority lead, but a precise primary passage is needed for a new verified edge.
emile_durkheim|Latour, Bourdieu, Meillet, Bloor, Radcliffe-Brown and Parsons already provide different responses and debts. Mauss/Hubert and school contexts cannot be collapsed into Durkheim alone.
sigmund_freud|Lacan, Fanon, Schapiro and Ginzburg distinguish resources, criticism and comparison. Clinical historical claims are not established psychological facts; the broader tradition includes other actors.
samuel_huntington|Modernization and Said's criticism exist. Tilly's 1973 response is distinct and promising, but only indexed primary-page text was accessible; full-page verification is held before release.
giorgio_agamben|State exception and a Foucault methodological resource exist. Homo Sacer explicitly proposes correcting/completing biopolitics, a distinct reverse response; retain its theoretical status and democracy/totalitarianism qualification.
theodor_w_adorno|Existing field routes correctly credit Horkheimer. The individually authored 1936 Benjamin letter is a different response and must not become a generic Frankfurt verdict.
charles_taylor|Recognition, cultural comparison and intellectual history exist. Habermas on multiculturalism requires his actual argument, not editorial co-presence in a collection.
'''
assessments=dict(l.split('|',1) for l in notes.strip().splitlines())
assert set(assessments)=={i['node']['id'] for i in context}
rows=[]
for i in context:
 rows.append({'node_id':i['node']['id'],'existing_edge_count':len(i['existing_edges']),'current_edge_ids':[e['id'] for e in i['existing_edges']], 'reviewed_entry_fields':['entry description and qualifications','incoming/outgoing relationship claims and evidence notes','selected-work context where present'], 'source_inventory_ids':[s['id'] for s in i['sources']], 'reviewed_source_ids':[], 'assessment':assessments[i['node']['id']], 'proposal_ids':[], 'held_case_ids':[], 'review_extent':'Entry and incident-relationship semantic screen with selective inherited verification-note review; not fresh reading of every source or complete oeuvre.', 'next_step':'Implement only independently checked linked proposals; investigate linked holds before any further addition.'})
write('node-review.json',{'baseline_revision':'1.191','lane':'debates','reviewer':'Astra','reviewed_on':'2026-10-25','scope_note':'All 90 assigned entries screened. Inventory is not a source-reading claim; low degree is not proof of incompleteness. Fresh readings are separately documented.','nodes':rows})
new_sources=[]; addenda=[]; proposals=[]
def witness(id,title,url,file,extent,limits,existing=False):
 p=Path('/tmp/astra-response-debates')/file
 record={'id':id,'title':title,'url':url,'verification':{'status':'supporting_page_checked','checked_on':'2026-10-25','note':extent+' '+limits},'witness_sha256':hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'raw_text_policy':'Private downloaded witness; no full text included in repository or browser assets.'}
 if existing: addenda.append({'source_id':id,'addendum_date':'2026-10-25','append_only_note':record['verification']['note'],'witness_url':url,'witness_sha256':record['witness_sha256'],'preserve_old_verification_verbatim':True})
 else: new_sources.append(record)
 return id
certeau=witness('certeau_practice_primary','Michel de Certeau, The Practice of Everyday Life, translated by Steven Rendall (English 1984; French original 1980), chapter IV.', 'https://notesoncraft.org/assets/pdfs/De-Certeau_The-Practice-of-Everyday-Life.pdf','certeau.pdf','Fresh selected English primary pages 50, 55–57 and 59–60 read by OCR of the scanned pages, especially section 2, Docta ignorantia: Bourdieu.','This extends rather than replaces the existing preface/introduction reading. The complete book, original French and Bourdieu originals were not reread. His account of Bourdieu is attributed to de Certeau, including praise for the descriptions.',True)
brubaker=witness('rt186_brubaker_identity','Rogers Brubaker and Frederick Cooper, Beyond Identity, Theory and Society 29:1 (2000), 1–47.','https://voidnetwork.gr/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/Beyond-Identity-by-Rogers-Brubaker-and-Frederick-Cooper.pdf','brubaker.pdf','Fresh primary printed pp. 8–9 and p. 40 note 28 read from the article reproduction.','Equal coauthor credit belongs to Frederick Cooper. Hall and Omi/Winant are the authors’ specific targets; their cited originals and the historical studies in note 28 were not independently reread. Existing selected-page reading limitations remain.',True)
arendt=witness('rea_debates_arendt_human_condition','Hannah Arendt, The Human Condition (1958), second edition with Margaret Canovan introduction (Chicago, 1998).','https://www.dbu.edu/Mitchell/modern-resources/_documents/arendt_the_human_condition.pdf','arendt.pdf','Selected primary printed pp. 79, 85–86, 88 and 101–104 read; second-edition imprint checked.','The argument is from 1958, not a new 1998 intervention. Arendt respects Marx’s intellectual achievement and acknowledges labor-power analysis while criticizing the labor/work and emancipation formulation. Complete book and cited Marx originals not independently collated.')
butler=witness('rea_debates_butler_gender_trouble','Judith Butler, Gender Trouble (1990), 1999 Routledge edition, chapter III section ii.','https://transreads.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/judith-butler-gender-trouble-feminism-and-the-subversion-of-identity-second-edition.pdf','butler.pdf','Selected primary printed pp. 119–120 and 122–126 read, Foucault, Herculine, and the Politics of Sexual Discontinuity; imprint distinguishes 1990 original, 1999 edition and 2002 ebook.','These page locators belong to the 1999 edition, not a collated 1990 printing. Butler’s productive use of Foucault is retained alongside this specific criticism. Complete book, original Barbin memoir and Foucault originals were not newly reread.')
ginzburg=witness('rea_debates_ginzburg_cheese_preface','Carlo Ginzburg, The Cheese and the Worms, translated by John and Anne Tedeschi (English 1980; Italian original 1976), preface to the Italian edition.','https://memoof.me/download/292/pdf/292.pdf','ginzburg.pdf','Selected primary printed pp. xvii–xix read by OCR of the scan; title and copyright pages checked for translators, 1976 original, 1980 English and 1992 Hopkins Paperbacks manifestation.','Ginzburg credits attention to exclusions while criticizing the treatment of excluded people and refusal of interpretation. The original Italian, French Foucault works and Pierre Riviere collection were not independently read for this audit; this is Ginzburg’s attributed interpretation.')
agamben=witness('rea_debates_agamben_homo_intro','Giorgio Agamben, Homo Sacer: Sovereign Power and Bare Life, translated by Daniel Heller-Roazen (English 1998; Italian original 1995), introduction.','https://www.thing.net/~rdom/ucsd/biopolitics/HomoSacer.pdf','agamben_text.pdf','Selected primary introduction in reformatted English reproduction, digital/retyped pp. 10–13, read; especially the explicit proposal to correct or complete Foucault’s biopolitics.','These are reproduction locators, not collated Stanford print page numbers. English credit/date corroborated by Indiana University catalogue https://iucat.iu.edu/iuc/1210603 . The text expressly does not flatten historical differences between democracy and totalitarianism. This is Agamben’s theoretical proposal, not an established universal history. Complete book, Italian original and Foucault originals not newly read.')
adorno=witness('rea_debates_adorno_benjamin_letter','Theodor W. Adorno, letter to Walter Benjamin, 18 March 1936, English collection Aesthetics and Politics, selected pp. 120–124.','https://platypus1917.org/wp-content/uploads/adornotobenjamin_03181936.pdf','adorno.pdf','Selected primary letter pages 120–124 read by OCR of rotated scanned spreads; heading establishes the letter date.','The 1936 intervention is distinct from its later English collection. No sole translator credit is inferred from the collection editor. Adorno acknowledges a shared materialist project and potential in both autonomous and mass art. Complete correspondence, following 1938 letter and Benjamin’s original essay were not newly collated.')
spivak=witness('spivak_speak','Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, Can the Subaltern Speak? (1988), abridged teaching reproduction.','https://jan.ucc.nau.edu/~sj6/Spivak%20CanTheSubalternSpeak.pdf','spivak.pdf','Fresh university-hosted abridged primary excerpt, reproduced printed pp. 24–25 and 27, read for the explicit Foucault-Deleuze conversation critique.','This excerpt’s anthology pagination is not the 1988 original or the previously read 1993 reprint. The old source’s fuller 1993 reading and all qualifications are preserved. The original conversation and all Deleuze/Guattari works were not independently reread. Her objection concerns representation and international labor, not literal inability to utter words.',True)
pratt=witness('rt184_pratt_contact','Mary Louise Pratt, Arts of the Contact Zone, Profession (1991), 33–40, Contact and Community section.','https://writing101.net/flip/wp-content/resources/documents/pratt_arts_of_the_contact_zone.pdf','pratt.pdf','Fresh selected digital pp. 4–5, Contact and Community, read after the previously documented opening pp. 1–3.','The reproduction’s digital pagination is not original journal pagination. The explicit body discussion of Anderson supports analytical appropriation; a malformed duplicated footnote in this reproduction is not relied on. Pratt does not accuse Anderson of believing actual nations homogeneous. Complete Anderson original and all Pratt works not newly collated.',True)
def prop(slug,source,target,kind,claim,evidence,sids,date,related=()):
 proposals.append({'proposal_id':'rea_debates_'+slug,'status':'primary_supported_pending_independent_crosscheck','source':source,'target':target,'type':'critique' if kind=='critique' else 'connection','relationship_kind':kind,'directed':True,'relationship':claim,'evidence_note':evidence,'source_ids':sids,'argument_date':date,'existing_related_edge_ids':list(related),'priority':'substantive_missing_named_response' if kind=='critique' else 'explicit_named_analytical_resource','preservation':'Additive only. Preserve all prior edge IDs, claims, source records and qualifications. No new biography or inferred universal school voice.'})
prop('certeau_bourdieu','michel_de_certeau','pierre_bourdieu','critique','De Certeau praises Bourdieu’s descriptions of practical ingenuity but questions whether habitus and structural reproduction suppress the tactical inventiveness they reveal.','Chapter IV, selected English pp. 50, 55–57, 59–60. This is de Certeau’s critical reading, including admiration for the descriptions, not a settled verdict on all Bourdieu. Complete books and original French remain unread for this audit.',[certeau],'1980 French argument; 1984 English translation')
prop('brubaker_cooper_hall','rogers_brubaker','stuart_hall','critique','With Frederick Cooper, Brubaker questions Hall’s claim that identity remains analytically indispensable and proposes more differentiated terms for identification and group formation.','Beyond Identity, pp. 8–9. The jointly authored criticism explicitly recognizes Hall’s sophisticated anti-essentialism; it does not characterize him as a simple essentialist or deny the political legitimacy of identity claims. Cooper retains equal authorship. One joint argument is projected here, not two independent studies.',[brubaker],2000)
prop('brubaker_cooper_racial_formation','rogers_brubaker','racial_formation','critique','With Frederick Cooper, Brubaker argues that Omi and Winant’s sharp ethnic/racial-group distinction insufficiently accommodates historical racialization of European immigrants and subsequent changes in racial categories.','Beyond Identity, p. 40 note 28, specifically discussing Omi and Winant’s 1994 formulation. The authors acknowledge its constructivist definition but challenge this distinction; the edge is not a verdict on every racial-formation historian. Their cited historical studies were not independently reread. Cooper retains equal authorship.',[brubaker],2000)
prop('arendt_marx','hannah_arendt','karl_marx','critique','Arendt challenges Marx’s elevation of labor and conflation of labor with work, identifying a tension between labor as distinctively human and emancipation from necessity.','The Human Condition, selected pp. 79, 85–86, 88, 101–104 in the 1998 second edition. The argument dates to 1958. Arendt’s respect for Marx and recognition of labor-power analysis qualify this particular criticism; cited Marx originals were not independently collated.',[arendt],1958)
prop('butler_foucault','judith_butler','foucault','critique','Butler argues that Foucault’s presentation of Herculine Barbin romanticizes pleasures outside power, conflicting with his own account of sexuality’s historical production.','Gender Trouble, chapter III ii, 1999-edition pp. 119–120, 122–126. This is a specific immanent criticism alongside productive use of Foucault, not rejection of his entire project. Barbin’s memoir and Foucault originals were not newly read; this edition’s pagination is not silently assigned to the 1990 original.',[butler],'1990 work; read in 1999 edition')
prop('ginzburg_foucault','carlo_ginzburg','foucault','critique','Ginzburg questions Foucault’s privileging of exclusion over excluded people, arguing that hostile or distorted records can still support inquiry into subordinate culture.','The Cheese and the Worms, preface to the Italian edition, pp. xvii–xix in the English translation. Ginzburg credits the attention to exclusion before criticizing the Pierre Riviere collection’s refusal of interpretation. This is his attributed reading; the French originals and collection were not independently collated. John and Anne Tedeschi share translation credit.',[ginzburg],'1976 Italian preface; 1980 English translation',('edge_259','edge_268'))
prop('agamben_foucault','giorgio_agamben','foucault','critique','Agamben proposes to correct or complete Foucault’s biopolitics by placing bare life’s sovereign exclusion in an older political structure and treating its generalized exception as distinctive of modernity.','Homo Sacer introduction, reformatted English digital pp. 10–13, especially p. 12. This is a theoretical corrective, not a proven universal historical sequence. The author explicitly disavows flattening the historical differences between democracy and totalitarianism. This differs from the existing Foucault-to-Agamben methodological resource. Stanford print pagination and full originals were not collated.',[agamben],'1995 Italian original; 1998 English translation',('edge_1568',))
prop('adorno_benjamin','theodor_w_adorno','walter_benjamin','critique','Adorno questions Benjamin’s weighting of autonomous and technologically reproduced art, arguing that both contain domination and possibilities of change rather than assigning emancipation to mass reception alone.','Letter of 18 March 1936, selected English collection pp. 120–124. Adorno acknowledges their common materialist aims and Benjamin’s contribution while criticizing this weighting. Letter date is not the English collection publication date. The complete correspondence, Benjamin essay and following 1938 letter were not newly collated.',[adorno],'1936-03-18')
prop('spivak_deleuze','gayatri_chakravorty_spivak','gilles_deleuze','critique','Spivak questions Deleuze’s account of oppressed subjects speaking transparently for themselves, insisting that representation and the international division of labour cannot be bypassed.','Can the Subaltern Speak?, fresh abridged teaching excerpt pp. 24–25 and 27, alongside the preserved fuller 1993-reprint reading in the source record. The target is his position in the conversation with Foucault, not every Deleuze/Guattari work. Preserve the existing Foucault response; the two named interlocutors are not interchangeable.',[spivak],1988,('edge_985',))
prop('anderson_pratt','benedict_anderson','mary_louise_pratt','influence','Pratt uses Anderson’s account of imagined communities to question homogeneous models of speech communities and distinguish them from unequal contact zones.','Arts of the Contact Zone, Contact and Community, selected digital pp. 4–5 of the reformatted reproduction (not original journal page numbers). This is explicit analytical appropriation. Pratt does not claim Anderson believes real nations homogeneous, and this is not a reverse critique of Anderson. Broken reproduction footnote is not used as evidence.',[pratt],1991)
